<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Xishuangbanna</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sipsong Panna, Sipsongpanna, Xishuangbanna Dai Autonomous Prefecture</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Xishuangbanna, in Tai Sipsongpanna, 'twelve thousand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> fields', is a region in the far south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> and Myanmar, now the Xishuangbanna Dai Autonomous Prefecture. It was the home of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tai-lu-people">Tai Lue</a> kingdom centred on Chiang Hung (Jinghong), which paid tribute to China and to Burmese kings and had close ties with Lan Na and Laos. Chinese administration replaced its hereditary rulers in the 1950s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xishuangbanna.jpg" alt="Xishuangbanna" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chlukoe · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jinghongcity.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Xishuangbanna lies in the extreme south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>, on the northern edge of the tropics, bordering Myanmar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>. The prefectural seat, Jinghong, is the largest settlement and straddles the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a>, called the Lancang River in Chinese. The prefecture lies at a lower altitude than most of Yunnan, with a tropical savanna climate. The name Sibsongpanna is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tai-lu-people">Tai Lue</a> compound of sipsong, twelve, pan, township, and na, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> paddy, and refers to the traditional division of the mueang into twelve districts. Its people, architecture and language resemble those of the Shan, Dai and Tai peoples, including the Thai and Lao, more than those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before 1886 the region was called Chiang Hung, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tai-lu-people">Tai Lue</a> kingdom contended between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, Lanna and the Burmese dynasties. After the Mongol conquest in 1296, the Ming and Qing emperors appointed the Tai Lue rulers as native Tusi governors. In 1563 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> captured Chiang Hung, and Burma and China then agreed on joint domination, the ruler being enthroned at a ceremony that Burmese and Chinese representatives jointly presided over. In the 19th century it briefly became a tributary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luang-prabang">Luang Prabang</a> and the Rattanakosin Kingdom during the Burmese-Siamese War of 1849-1855. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burma">Upper Burma</a> in 1886 it became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">British Raj</a>, and in 1892 the British transferred Chiang Hung to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>.</p><p>The Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> sent troops in 1913 to oust a local rebel, the Chao Maha of Meng Jie, and Ke Shuxun governed under his 13 Principles of Governing the Frontier. The Communists took control in 1952. On 23 January 1953 the People's Republic established the Xishuangbanna Dai Autonomous Region and ended the native-chieftain system, and in 1955 it became an autonomous prefecture. Land reform began in January 1956. Buddhist temples used as barns in the Cultural Revolution were restored to their purpose in 1981.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The prefecture is the home of the Dai people. A New Tai Lue alphabet, based on the Tai Tham alphabet, was created in 1953 to print material in Tai Lue. The Dai New Year, the Water-Splashing Festival, runs from 13 to 15 April. The region holds much of the biodiversity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> and the last few Asian elephants in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, protected in a reserve. Its six <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> mountains produce Pu-erh tea.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Xishuangbanna Dai Autonomous Prefecture” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Tai Tham script spread from Chiang Mai to the Tai Lue of <b>Sipsong Panna</b> in Yunnan, the Tai Khun of Kengtung in the Shan State and Lan Xang, where it became the standard script for Buddhist texts.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lan-na">Lan Na · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>During the succession conflicts after Sourigna Vongsa's death, his grandson Kingkitsarath, with backing from <b>Sipsongpanna</b>, established himself at Luang Prabang in 1707 as king, separate from the court of the Kingdom of Vientiane.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-luang-phrabang">Kingdom of Luang Phrabang</a></li><li><blockquote>Its lords were linked by marriage to Chiang Hung in <b>Sipsongpanna</b> and to Phayao.</blockquote><a href="/state/ngoenyang">Ngoenyang</a></li><li><blockquote>The later name <b>Sipsong Panna</b> means 'twelve thousand rice fields', referring to its administrative districts.</blockquote><a href="/state/sipsong-panna">Sipsong Panna</a></li></ul></section>
